1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,960
This project is about losing a region on purpose and measuring what it costs.

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:15,492
The same Go service, a small money-transfer ledger, runs in two regions on Cloud Run, Northern Virginia and South Carolina, behind one anycast IP on the global load balancer.

3
00:00:15,492 --> 00:00:25,660
Underneath there is one multi-region Cloud Spanner database: both regions hold a synchronously committed read-write replica and a witness in a third region breaks ties.

4
00:00:25,660 --> 00:00:41,093
So a regional outage changes where requests are served, never what the ledger says: the recovery point is zero by construction, and the recovery time is the load balancer's outlier detection evicting the dead region, which we measure rather than estimate.

5
00:00:41,093 --> 00:00:54,348
Transfers are serializable Spanner transactions keyed by a client idempotency key, reads come strong, stale or as of a past timestamp, and everything is Terraform with a keyless pipeline that rolls one region at a time.

6
00:00:55,757 --> 00:00:57,572
One IP, two regions, one ledger.

7
00:00:57,572 --> 00:01:07,777
Every response carries the region that served it; from where I am, the load balancer picks the nearest healthy one, and both regions answer the same question with the same numbers.

8
00:01:07,777 --> 00:01:19,853
Calling each region's Cloud Run URL directly shows the two deployments are identical, down to the revision, and the ledger summary is the invariant we will keep checking: twenty accounts, twenty thousand in total.

9
00:01:21,459 --> 00:01:23,257
Consistency under concurrency.

10
00:01:23,257 --> 00:01:34,287
Twenty workers fire keyed transfers through the edge for twenty seconds; some land in one region, some in the other, and all of them are serializable transactions on the same database.

11
00:01:34,287 --> 00:01:41,780
Afterwards the total balance is exactly what it was and the transfer count grew by exactly the number of successful requests.

12
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:46,515
Money moved, none was created, none was lost, with two regions writing at once.

13
00:01:48,124 --> 00:01:50,599
Three ways to read, and one way to retry.

14
00:01:50,599 --> 00:01:54,462
A strong read goes to the leader and returns the freshest value.

15
00:01:54,462 --> 00:02:02,853
A bounded-stale read is allowed to be up to fifteen seconds old and is served by the nearest replica, which is what a dashboard should use.

16
00:02:02,853 --> 00:02:11,908
And a transfer retried with the same idempotency key, say after a failover cut the first response off, comes back as a duplicate: nothing moves twice.

17
00:02:13,512 --> 00:02:22,652
The database in the console: instance configuration nam3, Enterprise Plus, a hundred processing units, which is a tenth of a node and plenty for this.

18
00:02:22,652 --> 00:02:26,674
Two read-write replicas, one per serving region, plus the witness.

19
00:02:26,674 --> 00:02:32,280
The default leader sits in Northern Virginia; we will move it later with a single statement.

20
00:02:34,387 --> 00:02:36,176
Drill one: a region goes dark.

21
00:02:36,176 --> 00:02:41,723
A probe hits the edge four times a second and a writer commits one keyed transfer per second.

22
00:02:41,723 --> 00:02:47,508
Then I flip the outage switch on Northern Virginia, so every request there answers five-oh-three.

23
00:02:47,508 --> 00:02:53,830
The edge fails for a few seconds, outlier detection ejects the region, and South Carolina takes all of it.

24
00:02:53,830 --> 00:02:56,335
That window is the measured recovery time.

25
00:02:56,335 --> 00:02:59,019
Then the region heals and traffic flows back.

26
00:02:59,019 --> 00:03:05,162
The recovery point: every keyed write is in the ledger exactly once and the total balance did not move.

27
00:03:05,162 --> 00:03:08,323
That is what one database under two regions buys you.

28
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:12,399
Drill two: where do writes commit?

29
00:03:12,399 --> 00:03:18,967
Spanner keeps the leader replicas in one region, and a write from the other region pays a cross-region round trip.

30
00:03:18,967 --> 00:03:25,880
Write latency from each region, then one DDL statement moves the default leader to South Carolina, and the numbers swap.

31
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:32,736
In a real failover Spanner elects a new leader on its own within seconds; this is the dial for when you want to choose.

32
00:03:34,347 --> 00:03:37,569
Drill three is the one replication cannot save you from.

33
00:03:37,569 --> 00:03:44,187
An operator runs an update that zeroes every balance, and the replicas faithfully copy the mistake in milliseconds.

34
00:03:44,187 --> 00:03:55,524
Spanner's version history does what replication cannot: a read as of a timestamp before the mistake still shows the right ledger, and the restore endpoint writes that state back in a single commit.

35
00:03:55,524 --> 00:04:00,243
Seconds, not a backup restore; the daily backup schedule stays as the second line.

36
00:04:01,844 --> 00:04:10,582
The dashboard splits the edge by serving region: requests and five-hundreds per region, p95 per region, Cloud Run instances, Spanner CPU and API latency.

37
00:04:10,582 --> 00:04:16,578
The outage shows up as a short burst of errors in one region and the other region's line taking the load.

38
00:04:16,578 --> 00:04:29,828
The SLOs are measured at the edge, the way a user would: ninety-nine point nine percent non-five-hundred, and ninety-five percent under five hundred milliseconds, each with burn-rate alerts and an uptime check from three continents.

39
00:04:31,933 --> 00:04:45,255
Delivery is keyless and region-aware: GitHub Actions tests, builds, pushes by digest, and rolls that digest to South Carolina first, then Northern Virginia, one at a time, so a bad build can never take both regions down in the same minute.

40
00:04:45,255 --> 00:04:50,941
Terraform owns everything around the revisions and deliberately ignores the image and the outage knob.

41
00:04:52,557 --> 00:05:10,623
Everything shown is in the repository: the Go service with its three read modes and idempotent transfers, Terraform for Spanner, Cloud Run, the global load balancer with its eviction policy, SLOs, alerts and dashboard, the three drill scripts, the load generator and the keyless workflow.

42
00:05:10,623 --> 00:05:15,830
The only idle cost is the Spanner instance, and the teardown script removes it all.

43
00:05:15,830 --> 00:05:17,085
Thanks for watching.
